course: everyday
lesson: 261
title: Pocket money, saving and spending
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Pocket money is kept somewhere safe, and you keep track of how much you have, so you always know what you can afford; small amounts put regularly into a money box add up over time.
explain: A children's savings account at a bank pays interest, which is extra money the bank gives you for keeping your money there.
explain: Before you buy, ask whether you really want the thing or only want to fit in, whether you would use both items in a «buy one get one free» offer, and whether a cheap toy will break and cost more in the end.
explain: Loot boxes and mystery packs are like gambling, because you pay without knowing what you get; and anyone online who asks you for a gift card or money is almost certainly a scammer.
explain: A girl in Texas sees a $10 toy and brings exactly $10, but in most US states sales tax is added at the till, so she needs a little more; when the toy turns out broken in the box, she takes it back with the receipt.
fact: Pocket money is kept in a safe place, such as a money box or purse, and you keep track of how much you have.
fact: If you ask for more pocket money, you explain calmly why and accept the answer, because parents plan for the whole family.
fact: Putting money in a money box regularly, without opening it, lets small amounts add up: £1 a week for a year (52 weeks) makes £52.
fact: Interest is extra money a bank pays you for keeping your money in a savings account.
fact: For a long savings goal, you celebrate halfway and keep going.
fact: Things bought only to fit in often bring little joy.
fact: A «buy one get one free» offer is only a bargain if you would use both.
fact: Paying a little more for something that lasts can cost less in the end than cheap things that break.
fact: Loot boxes and mystery packs are like gambling: you pay without knowing what you will get.
fact: You never send a gift card or money to someone online; scammers ask for gift cards because they cannot be traced (nobody can follow where the money went).
fact: You keep the receipt until you are sure an item works, and a faulty item can be taken back to be exchanged or refunded (given your money back).
fact: In most US states the shelf price does not include sales tax, which is added at the till.
exercise: You get $5 pocket money every week. Where should you keep it? | In a money box or purse, keeping track of it | Loose in your coat pocket | Under a stone in the garden | In your school desk
exercise: You want more pocket money. What is the best way to ask? | Explain calmly why, and accept the answer | Sulk until your parents give in | Demand it loudly in front of guests so they cannot say no | Take it from the family purse
exercise: How does a piggy bank help you save? | Small amounts added regularly add up over time | It makes each coin inside worth a little more | It pays you interest every week | It stops prices going up
exercise: Your money is in a children's savings account. What is interest? | Extra money the bank pays you for keeping it there | A fee you pay to the bank each month | Money the bank borrows from you and never returns | A prize for opening it
exercise: You are saving $60 for a scooter and have reached $30. What helps you keep going? | Celebrate being halfway and carry on | Spend the $30 because it is taking too long | Start a new goal instead | Give up because it is only half
exercise: Everyone in your class has a fidget toy, so you want one too. What should you ask yourself? | Do I really want it, or just want to fit in? | How fast can I get one before they go out of fashion? | Which is the most expensive one? | Who will notice if I have none?
exercise: A shop offers "buy one get one free" on a drink you do not like. Is it a bargain for you? | No, not if you would not use both | Yes, because anything free is always a good deal | Yes, because you save money | No, because offers are illegal
exercise: You can buy a $3 toy car that breaks in a day or a $7 one that lasts for years. Which is better value? | Probably the $7 one that lasts | Always the $3 one, because it is cheaper | Neither, as all toys break in the end | Buy five of the $3 cars instead
exercise: What is a loot box in a game? | A pay-for box where you do not know what you get | A free gift that the game gives to its best players | A box of real treasure | A safe place to keep your coins
exercise: Someone in an online game asks you to buy a gift card and send them the code. What do you do? | Never send it; tell a parent straight away | Send it if they promise to pay you back the next day | Send a small one to be nice | Ask them to prove who they are first
exercise: You buy new headphones. Why keep the receipt? | To return or exchange them if they do not work | It is good luck to keep receipts | The shop will ask to see it at the door as you leave | You need it to switch them on
exercise: You open a new toy at home and it is broken in the box. What do you do? | Take it back with the receipt for an exchange or refund | Throw it away and forget it | Glue it together yourself | Keep it, since you can never get your money back once a box is opened
exercise: In California, a book's shelf price is $10. Why might you need more than $10? | Sales tax is added at the till | Books always cost double at the till | The cashier adds a tip for herself | The price changes every single hour
exercise: Why do scammers ask for gift cards rather than cash? | Gift cards cannot be traced | Gift cards are worth more than cash | Gift cards are a polite present | Banks give gift cards for free
test: A boy in Leeds puts £1 a week in his money box for a year without opening it. Roughly how much will he have? | About £52 | About £12 | About £5 | About £100
test: A girl in New York has exactly $10 for a $10 board game. At the till she is told the total is $10.89. Why? | Sales tax is added to the shelf price | The cashier made a mistake when adding it up | The price went up while she walked | She must pay to use the till
test: A boy in Brisbane spends $40 on mystery card packs and gets mostly cards he already has. What does this show? | Mystery packs are like gambling: you never know what you get | He was just unlucky this once | Mystery packs always give the best cards | He should buy more packs, as the good cards must come up soon
test: A "friend" you met in an online game says, "Buy me a $50 gift card and I'll give you rare items." What is most likely? | It is a scam, so tell a parent and do not buy it | It is a fair trade between gamers | The items will be worth more than $50 | Gift cards are a safe way to trade
test: Why might paying more for a sturdy pair of trainers save money? | They last longer than several cheap pairs | Expensive things never break or wear out at all | Cheap shoes are not allowed at school by the rules | The shop gives a refund when they wear out
test: Two weeks after buying a torch, Mia finds it does not switch on. What helps her most now? | The receipt she kept from the shop | Buying a new torch quickly | Asking a friend to fix it | Writing a letter to the battery maker
test: Your older cousin asks for $20 more pocket money a week and is told no, because the family is saving for a car. What should he do? | Accept the answer calmly | Sulk until they change their minds | Take the money from the jar | Stop doing any chores until they agree
test: A girl in Ottawa wants the same expensive water bottle as her friends, though hers works fine. What is a wise thought? | Things bought only to fit in often bring little joy | She must buy it or she will lose all her friends | Expensive things always make you happy | Her bottle will break because it is cheap
test: In a savings account, $100 grows to $102 after a year without anyone adding money. Where did the extra $2 come from? | Interest paid by the bank | A mistake by the bank | A tax refund | The money grew by itself in the vault
test: mum, can u lend me 10 dollars for gift cards, a guy online says he'll double it | No, it's a scam, never send gift cards online | Yes, it's a good deal | Yes, if he sends a photo of the money first | Send just five dollars to test it
test: buy 1 get 1 free on these crisps, but i only like one bag tbh | It's only a bargain if you'd use both | Always buy it, free is free | Buy four to get more free | It's cheaper to buy none
test: new console broke after 2 days, threw the box n receipt away lol | Keep the receipt till you know it works | Receipts never matter for big things like consoles | You can never return a faulty thing | Shops pay you to keep broken things
test: everyone at school has these trainers, gonna spend all my savings on em | Things bought just to fit in often bring little joy | Yes, fitting in is worth every penny you have | Spend it all, saving is pointless | Buy two pairs to fit in more
explain: A girl in Texas called Ana spent all her pocket money on sweets every week; when her bike tyre burst she had nothing saved and walked to school for a month.
explain: A boy in Ireland gave his savings to an older boy online who promised to "double it by Friday"; the older boy blocked him, and the money was gone for good.
fact: Spending everything at once leaves nothing for the things that suddenly matter later.
fact: Anyone who promises to double your money fast is almost always running a trick, and the money is rarely got back.
test: got $10, gonna blow it all on sweets today | Save some for things you'll need later | Spend it all, money's for spending | Spend it, pocket money can't be saved anyway | Buy lottery tickets to win more
test: guy online says send £5, he'll send back £10 tmrw | Don't, it's a trick, tell an adult | Send it, it's only £5, not much to lose | Send it and ask him for proof afterwards | Send £10 instead so you get £20 back
test: Why did Ana have to walk to school for a month? | She had saved nothing to fix her bike | Bikes are not allowed at school | Her school moved further away from home | The shop had no tyres
test: did u hear a kid sent his whole birthday money in gift cards to a guy online who promised him a free game? real lesson? | Never send gift cards or money online; it's a scam | Send only half, to be safe | It's fine if he has a lot of followers | Gift cards are traceable, so it's always safe to send them
test: apparently some kid spent all his savings on loot boxes and got nothing good. what's the true lesson? | Loot boxes are like gambling; you don't know what you get | Keep buying until you win | Loot boxes are a smart savings plan | They always give good prizes eventually if you keep paying
test: my mate's cousin says his friend doubled his money in a week with some online thing. right? | Almost always a trick; the money is rarely got back | Yes, send money to try it | Yes, if they say it's guaranteed to work | Yes, doubling is easy online if you know the right site
exam: Identify the nature of interest paid upon a savings account. | Extra money paid by a bank for keeping money with it | A charge for withdrawing one's money | The price of opening a money box | A tax upon pocket money levied by the government each year
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a «buy one get one free» offer? | It is a bargain only if both items will be used | It is invariably a bargain | It always halves the price of everything in the basket | It is a bargain if the shop says so
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice in respect of a receipt. | It is retained until the item is known to work | It is discarded at the shop door | It is given back to the cashier | It is needed only for food and never for other goods
exam: Ascertain the reason for which scammers request gift cards. | Money on gift cards is very hard to trace or recover | Gift cards are cheaper than money | Gift cards are required by law | Gift cards earn the sender interest
